IE520 4-DEVICE STACK — TEST RESULTS
===================================
Date:       2026-09-04
Bench:      tb470 (consoles /dev/u2 /dev/u3 /dev/u4 /dev/u5)
Objective:  Determine whether four AT-IE520-28GSX units can form a single
            4-member VCStack (virtual chassis).

VERDICT:  NEGATIVE — the IE520 CANNOT form a 4-member stack.
          The product limits VCStack membership to a maximum of 2 (stack
          member IDs 1 and 2 only). A third or fourth member ID is rejected by
          the CLI on every unit tested. A 4-member stack is therefore not
          configurable on this platform.


TEST ENVIRONMENT
----------------
Product:           AT-IE520-28GSX
Software version:  tomahawk_ie520-continuous, build Sun Aug 30 23:47:03 UTC 2026
Boot:              IE520-tb470.rel (flash boot)
RAM (per unit, idle standalone):  Total 506728 kB, Free ~265000 kB (~52% free)

Units under test (identified by serial number; console names per tb470):
  intended member 1 (master)  console /dev/u2   S/N 264A23061
  intended member 2           console /dev/u3   S/N 264A23068
  intended member 3           console /dev/u4   S/N 264A23052
  intended member 4           console /dev/u5   S/N 264A23066

Common stack identity applied:  stack virtual-chassis-id 3439
Master priority:                stack 1 priority 1  (applied to S/N 264A23061)


BLOCKING LIMIT — logged CLI responses
-------------------------------------
The stack member ID cannot be set above 2. "stack renumber" to any ID > 2 is
rejected identically on every unit tested.

Device S/N 264A23052 (intended member 3), console /dev/u4:
    awplus(config)# stack 1 renumber 3
    % The max stack member ID supported by this product is 2
    awplus(config)# stack 1 renumber 4
    % The max stack member ID supported by this product is 2
    awplus(config)# stack 1 renumber 8
    % The max stack member ID supported by this product is 2

Device S/N 264A23066 (intended member 4), console /dev/u5:
    awplus(config)# stack 1 renumber 4
    % The max stack member ID supported by this product is 2
    awplus(config)# stack 1 renumber 8
    % The max stack member ID supported by this product is 2

Control: renumber to ID 2 IS accepted — device S/N 264A23068 was successfully
renumbered from ID 1 to member 2 and rebooted into that ID. This confirms the
ceiling is exactly 2, not a general renumber failure.


CORROBORATING EVIDENCE — provisioning also caps at 2
----------------------------------------------------
Provisioning a switch slot above 2 is rejected.

Device S/N 264A23052, console /dev/u4:
    awplus(config)# switch 3 provision ie520-28
    % Invalid switch value
    awplus(config)# switch 4 provision ie520-28
    % Invalid switch value

Two independent CLI paths (stack renumber, switch provision) both cap the
member/switch ID at 2.


CLI REFERENCE CHECK
-------------------
The ck.db AlliedWare Plus CLI reference contains 0 command rows for product
"ie520" (the IE520 is not documented there). "stackport" is documented for
x240, x250, x950, x908gen2, x908gen3, xs900mx — not IE520. The reference thus
neither confirms nor denies the limit; the running device is the authority, and
it reports max member ID 2 (logged above).


WHAT WAS ATTEMPTED TO OVERCOME THE LIMIT
----------------------------------------
  stack 1 renumber 3 / 4 / 8   -> rejected: "% The max stack member ID supported
                                  by this product is 2"   (S/N 264A23052 and 264A23066)
  switch 3 provision ie520-28  -> rejected: "% Invalid switch value"  (S/N 264A23052)
  switch 4 provision ie520-28  -> rejected: "% Invalid switch value"  (S/N 264A23052)

No CLI mechanism was found to raise the member/switch ID ceiling above 2.

Because 4 unique member IDs cannot be assigned, the 4-member build step
("no shutdown" on the stackports to bring the links up) was NOT performed: with
the IDs the product allows, the four cabled units carry duplicate member IDs,
which produces a duplicate-member-ID / "neighbor incompatible" collision (the
same failure previously seen when these units shared one chassis-id with
duplicate IDs), not a 4-member stack. Building it would only reproduce that
collision and disturb the bench, so it was not done.


KNOCK-ON ISSUES OBSERVED (IE520 stackport behaviour)
----------------------------------------------------
1. "shutdown" on a stackport is NOT saved to configuration and does not persist
   across a reboot:
       awplus(config-if)# shutdown
       % port1.0.27: Warning: "shutdown" command is not saved to configuration for stackports
   The port does go admin-down at runtime (show interface: "disabled"), but any
   reboot re-enables it. Consequence during multi-unit configuration: after a
   config reboot a unit's stackports come back up on their own and must be
   re-shut after every reboot to stop an uncabled/partially-cabled stack from
   forming.

2. Renumbering a unit to ID 2 renames its real ports from port1.0.x to port2.0.x;
   the old port1.0.27-28 become "provisioned" (phantom). A "shutdown" applied to
   the pre-renumber range strands on the phantom ports while the real stackports
   (port2.0.27-28) stay enabled. Observed on S/N 264A23068 after its renumber to
   member 2 — its real stackports had to be re-shut on the port2.0.x range.


FINAL STATE OF THE UNITS (left safe; every stackport admin-down)
----------------------------------------------------------------
  S/N 264A23061  /dev/u2  member ID 1  chassis-id 3439  priority 1  port1.0.27-28 disabled
  S/N 264A23068  /dev/u3  member ID 2  chassis-id 3439              port2.0.27-28 disabled
  S/N 264A23052  /dev/u4  member ID 1  chassis-id 3439              port1.0.27-28 disabled
  S/N 264A23066  /dev/u5  member ID 1  chassis-id 2675 (unchanged)  port1.0.27-28 disabled

All four units are standalone; no stack link is up. Stackport DAC cables are
present (show interface Type "1xCOPPER PAS") but every stackport is admin-down,
so cabling the units cannot form a stack until a stackport is brought up.

DURABILITY CAVEAT: the admin-down state is runtime-only and is NOT retained
across a reboot (knock-on issue 1). The units are cabled and are known to reboot
unattended. If two cabled units reboot and bring their stackports back up while
still carrying duplicate member ID 1 (u2/u4/u5), they will attempt to form and
hit the duplicate-member-ID collision. To make the shut state durable without a
reboot dependency, run "no stackport" on the stacking ports (persisted; requires
one reboot to take effect) or physically uncable the stackports.


MAXIMUM SUPPORTED CONFIGURATION
-------------------------------
A 2-member VCStack (member IDs 1 and 2) is the maximum this product allows:
renumber to ID 2 and provisioning of switch 2 are both accepted.
